The MINIX 2.0.4 licence, and the provenance of the files in this directory.

This file was previously a placeholder recording that nobody had traced where
this code came from.  That has now been done.  What follows is (1) the
provenance, established by comparison against the holding, and (2) the licence
text as it appears IN that distribution, reproduced verbatim.

This file reports what the distribution's own documents say.  It does not draw
conclusions about what may be done with the code; read the terms yourself.


1. Which release
----------------

MINIX 2.0.4.  Established from the donor holding at `c900oses/minix2/`, which
is the unpacked MINIX 2.0.4 source distribution plus its three original
`compress`ed tar archives (`SYS.TAZ`, `CMD.TAZ`, `FIX.TAZ`):

  - `minix2/sys/include/minix/config.h` lines 5-6:

        #define OS_RELEASE "2"
        #define OS_VERSION "0.4"

    The same two lines are present in the pristine `SYS.TAZ` archive at
    `include/minix/config.h`, so this is the distribution's own statement and
    not an artefact of unpacking.

  - This project's own record of the port names the same source: the MINIX
    2.0.4 userland inet server, `minix2/sys/src/inet/`.

  - File dates in `SYS.TAZ` run to 2002-12-22; `FIX.TAZ` is a later fix set
    dated 2003-11-30 (three kernel PCI/rtl8139 files), which does not touch
    `src/inet/`.

  - `minix2/sys/src/inet/version.c`, carried into this directory unchanged,
    identifies the stack itself as `inet 0.35K`.


2. The licence, as held
-----------------------

The distribution carries its licence at the TOP OF THE SOURCE TREE, in
`/usr/src/LICENSE` -- one directory above `/usr/src/inet/`, the directory this
one is a port of.  In the holding it is `minix2/sys/src/LICENSE`, and it is
present byte-identically inside the original `SYS.TAZ` archive as `src/LICENSE`
(dated 2001-01-02, md5 25d41a23c639100f13573c6532d3ca7e).

The distribution documents that file as the licence.  `minix2/sys/man/man7/
hier.7`, describing the MINIX filesystem layout, lists under `src/`:

        LICENSE
                Minix license to use

And per-file headers point at it by path.  `minix/const.h` -- the header this
port carries at `../include/minix/const.h`, and the file whose Prentice-Hall
notice prompted this investigation -- opens:

    /* Copyright (C) 2001 by Prentice-Hall, Inc.  See the copyright notice in
     * the file /usr/src/LICENSE.
     */

The full text of `minix2/sys/src/LICENSE`, reproduced verbatim:

        Copyright (c) 1987,1997,2001 Prentice Hall
        All rights reserved.

        Redistribution and use of the MINIX operating system in source and
        binary forms, with or without modification, are permitted provided
        that the following conditions are met:

           * Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright
             notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.

           * Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above
             copyright notice, this list of conditions and the following
             disclaimer in the documentation and/or other materials provided
             with the distribution.

           * Neither the name of Prentice Hall nor the names of the software
             authors or contributors may be used to endorse or promote
             products derived from this software without specific prior
             written permission.

        THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS, AUTHORS, AND
        CONTRIBUTORS ``AS IS'' AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES,
        INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF
        MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE DISCLAIMED.
        IN NO EVENT SHALL PRENTICE HALL OR ANY AUTHORS OR CONTRIBUTORS BE
        LIABLE FOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR
        CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF
        SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES; LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR PROFITS; OR
        BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND ON ANY THEORY OF LIABILITY,
        WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE
        OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE OF THIS SOFTWARE,
        EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.

That is the whole file; it is 32 lines and there is nothing else in it.  Note
what it does and does not say: it names Prentice Hall as the copyright holder
and speaks of "the MINIX operating system" as a whole and of "the software
authors or contributors" -- it does not enumerate files or authors.  It does
not mention the Vrije Universiteit, and it is not the older
educational/non-commercial MINIX 1.x-era licence that the previous version of
this file assumed.  Earlier MINIX releases were distributed on other terms;
this is the file that accompanies 2.0.4, and everything recorded here is about
2.0.4 only.


3. What Philip Homburg's 1995 notice is
---------------------------------------

Every file in `/usr/src/inet/` in the MINIX 2.0.4 distribution itself carries
the same bare notice our copies do, e.g. `minix2/sys/src/inet/buf.c`:

    /*
    This file contains routines for buffer management.

    Copyright 1995 Philip Homburg
    */

So the absence of terms in these headers is NOT something introduced by this
port, and these are not a standalone drop of code from outside a distribution.
It is how the files appear inside the MINIX 2.0.4 source tree, one level below
the `LICENSE` quoted above, in a distribution that places its permission
statement at the top of the tree, documents it there in `hier(7)`, and refers
to it from per-file headers by path.  Homburg's line is an authorship/copyright
notice of the kind the distribution uses throughout; the permission statement
for the tree is the `LICENSE` file.

Whether that top-level LICENSE reaches these particular files is a question
about the document, and this file does not answer it.  It records only that the
notice is a per-file authorship notice inside a distribution that carries a
top-level licence, rather than a standalone file with no terms anywhere.


4. Correspondence: our files against the holding
------------------------------------------------

Compared by content against `c900oses/minix2/sys/src/inet/`.  Of the 56 C,
header and assembler files in this directory:

  Byte-identical to MINIX 2.0.4 (35):
    buf.c  const.h  mq.c  mq.h  version.c
    generic/{arp.c arp.h buf.h clock.h eth.c eth.h eth_int.h event.c
             icmp.h icmp_lib.h io.c io.h ip.c ip.h ip_eth.c ip_int.h
             ip_ioctl.c ip_lib.c ip_ps.c ip_write.c psip.c psip.h sr.h
             tcp.h tcp_int.h tcp_lib.c tcp_recv.c type.h udp.c udp.h}

  MINIX 2.0.4 files, modified by this port (13):
    inet.h  inet_config.c  inet_config.h  osdep_eth.h  stacktrace.c
    generic/{assert.h event.h icmp.c ip_read.c ipr.c ipr.h tcp.c tcp_send.c}

  From MINIX 2.0.4 but a different directory (1):
    generic/oneC_sum.c  <- minix2/sys/src/lib/ip/oneC_sum.c, converted from
                           ANSI prototypes to K&R for the MWC compiler.

  Written for this port, not MINIX (7):
    coh_clock.c  coh_eth.c  coh_fdc.c  coh_inet.c  coh_osdep.c  coh_sr.c
    get_bp.s

  Present in MINIX 2.0.4 and deliberately NOT carried over (5):
    inet.c  clock.c  sr.c  mnx_eth.c  proto.h
    -- the MINIX message-passing osdep layer, replaced by the `coh_*` files
       above, which reach the drivers through COHERENT device calls instead of
       MINIX messages.

Nothing in this directory traces to any source other than MINIX 2.0.4 and this
port's own work.  The lineage is what it was believed to be.

The neighbouring `../include/` headers are the same lineage: 26 of 43 are
byte-identical to `minix2/sys/include/`, 12 more are modified copies of MINIX
2.0.4 headers, and 5 (`coh_fdc.h`, `inet_chan.h`, `inet_ipc.h`, `netdb.h`,
`arpa/inet.h`) are this port's.

Elsewhere under `net/`, material that is NOT under this licence and carries
its own:

  ../resolv/*.c, ../rcmd.c, ../ruserok.c
        Regents of the University of California, 1983/1985/1989.  These came
        via `minix2/sys/src/lib/ip/`, but each carries its own Berkeley notice
        in its own header; those notices govern.

  ../libsocket.c, ../netdb.c, ../inet_chan.c, ../sntp.c, ../coh_slip.c,
  ../coh_ppp.c, ../coh_ifconfig.c, ../rawsys.s and the test clients
        Written for this port.  Not MINIX.


5. What is still open
---------------------

  - This is the licence as it appears in the holding, which is a source
    distribution.  Nobody here has checked it against a MINIX 2.0.4 release
    announcement or any other upstream statement.  The presence of `FIX.TAZ`
    shows the holding is a distribution as shipped rather than a partial
    extract, and `SYS.TAZ` carries the LICENSE, so the file is very unlikely
    to be an accident -- but it has been read from one copy.

  - The MINIX distribution also contains third-party trees under their own
    separate terms (`cmd/src/commands/{bc,flex-2.3.7,ash/bltin}`,
    `sys/src/lib/regex`, `i386/mtools-3.9.7` each ship their own
    COPYING/LICENSE).  None of them is in this directory.
